    "create_time": 1772594160.036,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "**Case that tariffs raise prices:** A tariff is a tax on imports, typically paid by the importing firm. Empirical work on the 2018–19 US tariffs found near-complete pass-through to US import prices: foreign exporters mostly didn't cut prices, so US firms and consumers paid. Tariffs on intermediate goods (steel, components) also raise costs for domestic manufacturers, who pass some of that on. Domestic competitors facing less foreign competition can raise prices too.\n\n**Case that they don't cause *inflation*:** Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level, not a one-time change in relative prices. A tariff raises the price level once; unless it triggers ongoing wage-price dynamics or keeps escalating, measured inflation rises temporarily and then fades. A stronger dollar (tariffs can push the currency up) offsets some import costs, and central banks can tighten to keep expectations anchored.\n\n**Synthesis:** tariffs reliably raise the prices of affected goods; whether that becomes persistent inflation depends on their size, breadth, retaliation, and monetary policy response."
     ]
    },
